{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-140763",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "We wish to express $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{735}^{∘}$ in terms of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{\\theta }_{R},$ where ${\\theta }_{R}$ is the reference angle corresponding to the angle $\\theta ={735}^{∘}.$\n\nFirst, we find an angle that's coterminal with ${735}^{∘}$ that lies in the range $[0,{360}^{∘}).$ We do this by subtracting integer multiples of ${360}^{∘}$ until we get our desired angle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{735}^{∘}-{360}^{∘} & ={375}^{∘} \\\\ {735}^{∘}-2\\cdot {360}^{∘} & ={15}^{∘}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that our coterminal angle is acute and lies in the first quadrant. Therefore, ${\\theta }_{R}={15}^{∘},$ and we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡{735}^{∘}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡{15}^{∘}.\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14253971/assets/b21bfb70cc0716467232e2b5e67e7ae4e9ce0956ee80dc06a9d7e072009bf551.png)",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
